;;; `naf-upward-closure.ein` with the singleton writeback OFF — M1e2 **W3**'s
;;; lever row, where `dead` not being upward-closed under `absent` turns into a
;;; certified `Contradiction`. Register row `W3/naf-nowb-L`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; Below this header the program is `../naf-upward-closure.ein` with
;;; `:enable-singleton-writeback false` added to its `(config …)` line and
;;; nothing else; that file's header is the argument (Q-M1e.9, D4).
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   alive₀          = {(p A), (q A)}
;;;   {(p A)}         is dead   — `bad` fires, (q A) absent
;;;   {(p A), (q A)}  is alive  — the guard's witness is present
;;;
;;; so the one maximal state is **`{(p A), (q A)}`: `Solution`, k = 1.**
;;;
;;; ── What the engine says ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   default  Solution k = 1, `{(q A)}` — the same wrong model as the banked
;;;            file: the kill cache writes `(not (p A))` with no premises.
;;;   -L       `No solution — the constraints are contradictory`, unsat core
;;;            `(p A)`, `exhausted = true`. With neither the cache nor the
;;;            writeback, `{(p A)}` dies at layer 1 and its width-1 no-good
;;;            prunes the live superset as if it were dead. `{(q A)}` survives
;;;            and is not recorded either — `(p A)` is still proposed, so it is
;;;            not `complete` — and the empty layer 2 ends the run with the
;;;            dead core as the program's. Two mechanisms, then: W3's clause,
;;;            and W1's unrecorded survivor; a W1 fix alone would make this
;;;            answer the default run's wrong `{(q A)}`.
;;;
;;; No `:expect`: the two declared runs answer differently, and the register
;;; states both.
;;;
;;; Banked from M1e2's probes at S1e2.1.1.
;;;
;;; **Since M1e2 S1e2.1a.5 the program is `../naf-upward-closure.ein`'s.** The
;;; switch retired `:enable-singleton-writeback` (it wrote into root, which no
;;; route does), so only `:warn-derived-naf` was left on the `(config …)` line,
;;; until M1e2 S1e2.2.5 retired that flag too and the line went; R never read
;;; the writeback, and was the one it was.
;;;
;;; ── 2026-11-02 — *forbid* ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The user ruled *forbid* (Q-M1e2.5, M1e2 S1e2.4.1 § 5): `bad` concludes
;;; `(false)` from `(absent (q ?x))` over `q`, which a commitment can still
;;; extend, so the right answer to this program is a refusal at load, the
;;; error naming the replacement. Register row W3/naf-nowb-L, confirmed at the
;;; revisit (S1e2.1.7, 2026-11-03).
;;;
;;; ── 2026-11-04 — refused ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; M1e2 S1e2.2.5 built the refusal, as on `../naf-upward-closure.ein`, and
;;; the row is fixed: `ein solve` exits 1 naming `bad` over `q`. Before that
;;; R's maximal state was the `{(p A), (q A)}` above, what *respect* would have
;;; answered, kept as the row's alternative.

(relation is-a T T :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation p T :why "p holds of {?1}")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}")

(is-a A T)

(rule bad ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?x} has p without q")

(query
  :goal   (is-a ?x T))
